---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive';
const title = "Omnisend vs Attentive for Shopify: unified messaging or a split stack";
const desc = "Compare Omnisend and Attentive for Shopify email and SMS ownership, channel permissions, queued-message suppression and an evidence-based cost model.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can Attentive be evaluated for email too?",
    "answer": "Yes. Its developer listing advertises email and SMS. Verify required email workflows and package access in your account rather than assuming a second email service is mandatory."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does push automatically favor Omnisend?",
    "answer": "Omnisend advertises web push and Attentive describes cross-channel push audiences. Verify the specific push type, integration and eligibility instead of treating all push claims as equivalent."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should channels be split?",
    "answer": "When a demonstrated requirement justifies the extra identity, permission, event and suppression handoffs. Revenue thresholds alone do not establish that need."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Omnisend vs Attentive</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Omnisend vs Attentive for Shopify: unified messaging or a split stack</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Both suppliers advertise email and SMS. The useful comparison is whether your required customer journey works under one owner or a deliberate split. Store revenue alone cannot decide that architecture.</p>
      </header>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <p><em>Source descriptions checked November 24, 2026. Proposed exercises below are not completed tests or merchant results.</em></p>
        <h2>Correct the SMS-only premise</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/attentive">Attentive developer listing</a> describes email and SMS marketing and cross-channel audiences, including push and RCS. The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/omnisend">Omnisend listing</a> advertises email, SMS, web push, forms and automation. These are supplier descriptions, not verified entitlement in every region or account. Do not reject Attentive as an email candidate or claim it categorically lacks push from an old comparison.</p>
        <h2>Define a complete journey</h2>
        <p>Specify where signup happens, which channel permissions it records, the welcome sequence, cart or checkout entry, purchase suppression and postpurchase follow-up. Mark browser push permission separately from phone and email permission. Ask each supplier to demonstrate the exact required journey and channel availability instead of assuming a matching channel name means matching behavior.</p>
        <h2>Proposed exercise: purchase between channels</h2>
        <p>This proposed exercise has not been run here. Queue an email and a later text for an authorized test profile, then place an order between them. Inspect whether the purchase cancels the pending text and how quickly the event becomes eligible for workflow decisions. Repeat with email-only permission and an SMS opt-out. Record actual event and exclusion behavior rather than inventing a universal latency limit.</p>
        <h2>One platform still needs message governance</h2>
        <p>Check frequency limits, exclusions and operator permissions across promotional and automated sends. A single dashboard can still contain overlapping journeys. Have the person who owns campaigns change a recent-purchaser exclusion and inspect the resulting audience; record effort and mistakes without assigning a fictional score or a mandatory completion time.</p>
        <h2>If splitting channels, specify the boundary</h2>
        <p>For Omnisend email with Attentive SMS, document the source of subscriber identity, how purchase events reach both, who owns recovery, and how opt-outs affect each channel. A phone match is not sufficient proof of SMS permission. Keep transactional notices and customer support routing visible in the ownership inventory so a marketing migration does not silently disable them.</p>
        <h2>Check peak-send economics without invented ceilings</h2>
        <p>Model normal and peak recipient volumes, destination countries, message lengths and the required product package using current quotes. Ask about credits, limits, overages, support and termination terms. Do not attribute delivery problems to a universal Omnisend send ceiling or assume an Attentive contract minimum. Message segments and audience eligibility can change actual billed usage.</p>
        <h2>Measure the architecture, not competing dashboards</h2>
        <p>Define a common attribution window and account for overlapping channel claims before comparing reports. For an incremental-revenue question, design an appropriate holdout rather than adding each platform’s attributed orders together. Track operator workload and delivery exceptions alongside conversion. No merchant lift, compliance ranking or performance advantage is established by this page.</p>
        <h2>Acceptance and rollout</h2>
        <p>Keep a baseline of active welcome, recovery, postpurchase and winback ownership. Preserve exports and permission evidence, verify sender identity and destination links, and rehearse pausing queued messages. Choose unified operation if it meets the required workflows and support needs; split only when the demonstrated capability is worth maintaining the event and permission handoffs.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/attentive-vs-mailchimp">Attentive vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Omnisend vs Attentive FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
